MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: db176a60e3b58b4408636b22a97519c4, type: 3}
  m_Name: CommunicationBreakdownEvent
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MarsEvent
  eventTitle: Communication Breakdown
  eventDescription: Warning! The Mars outpost has lost its communication link with
    Earth due to a malfunction in the communication system. Without reliable contact,
    the crew cannot receive mission updates or technical assistance. You must decide
    how to restore communications while managing the base's limited resources.
  targetSol: 7
  optionATitle: Repair Communication Antenna
  energyChangeA: -10
  lifeSupportChangeA: 0
  shieldChangeA: 10
  resultTitleA: Communication Antenna Repaired
  resultDescriptionA: The crew successfully repairs the main communication antenna,
    restoring contact with Earth. Mission control can once again provide technical
    guidance and safety updates. The repair consumes energy, but improves the outpost's
    overall operational protection.
  optionBTitle: Activate Backup Communication
  energyChangeB: -5
  lifeSupportChangeB: -5
  shieldChangeB: 5
  resultTitleB: Backup Communication Activated
  resultDescriptionB: The crew activates the backup communication system and restores
    limited contact with Earth. The operation uses less energy than repairing the
    main antenna, but requires temporarily redirecting resources from life support
    systems. Basic mission coordination and safety monitoring are restored.
  educationalExplanation: Communication between Earth and Mars relies on radio signals
    transmitted through space. Because of the large distance between the planets,
    signals can take several minutes to travel in one direction. NASA uses the Deep
    Space Network to communicate with distant spacecraft. Reliable communication
    systems and autonomous operations are essential for future human missions to
    Mars.
  scientificSource: "NASA \u2014 Deep Space Network"
  scientificSourceURL: https://www.nasa.gov/directorates/somd/space-communications-navigation-program/deep-space-network/
